<ContentLayout
  title="Website Backlink Audit: Link Evidence, Risk and Reclamation"
  description="Review referring pages, document link-scheme evidence, recover broken destinations and decide whether Google disavow guidance applies to your site."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  publishDate="2026-03-28"
  updateDate="2026-09-11"
>
  <p>A website backlink audit reviews the external pages linking to your site, the destinations they use and any evidence of manipulative link building. Its deliverable is a documented action list: repair a broken destination, investigate a placement, preserve a useful link or take no action. A disavow file is not a routine audit deliverable.</p>
  <p>Start with your own link history and Search Console, then use an independent link index if it adds useful coverage. Keep observed facts separate from a tool's authority or toxicity score. For a wider traffic investigation, use the <a href="/seo-website-audit/rankings-drop/">rankings decline diagnosis</a> before attributing a loss to links.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-a-backlink-audit">What does a backlink audit examine?</h2>
  <p>The unit of review is a referring page and its link to a destination on your site. Domain summaries help triage large exports, but a domain can contain both relevant editorial pages and unrelated user submissions. Preserve the page-level record when deduplicating data.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> referring URL, publisher, surrounding text and review date.</li>
    <li><strong>Destination:</strong> linked URL, response status, redirect chain and final relevant page.</li>
    <li><strong>Relationship:</strong> editorial reference, directory entry, sponsorship, affiliate placement or an arrangement your team made.</li>
    <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> visible link text, link attributes, screenshot or export source and any known campaign records.</li>
    <li><strong>Decision:</strong> retain, repair, investigate or escalate, with a named owner and reason.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Review after a migration, a documented link campaign or a manual-action notice. Otherwise choose a cadence based on the site's changes and risk history rather than a mandatory monthly cleanup.</p>

  <h2 id="why-quality-matters">Assess relevance and provenance, not a universal score</h2>
  <p>A useful link helps a reader reach a relevant destination. Review the actual page, why the reference belongs there and whether the destination still answers the reader's need. Third-party domain scores can sort a queue; they are not Google's measurements and cannot establish the effect of an individual link on rankings.</p>
  <p>Language, country, low estimated traffic and an unfamiliar publisher do not by themselves establish spam. A new specialist publication can be relevant without a high domain score. A recognised domain can also host an unsuitable paid placement. Avoid fixed anchor-text percentages or domain-rating thresholds that turn context into a binary verdict.</p>

  <h2 id="pulling-data">Build a traceable link inventory</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Export external links from Search Console and retain the export date. Google's <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/9049606">Links report documentation</a> explains that the report is sampled, not a complete live inventory.</li>
    <li>If needed, add an export from your existing backlink research provider. Record the provider and first-seen or last-seen fields; those describe its crawler observations.</li>
    <li>Deduplicate identical source and destination pairs while retaining provider provenance. Keep multiple links from the same domain when their contexts differ.</li>
    <li>Inspect important destinations on your own site first. A broken product or research URL can be an actionable finding without judging the referring domain.</li>
    <li>Review a documented sample of remaining links, expanding it when a pattern warrants investigation. State how much of the inventory was actually checked.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Do not describe combined exports as every backlink on the web. Coverage and crawl dates differ, and a provider reporting a lost link is a prompt to recheck the source.</p>

  <h2 id="identifying-toxic-links">Investigate link-scheme evidence</h2>
  <p>Use <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies#link-spam">Google's link-spam policy</a> to assess arrangements intended to manipulate ranking. Relevant evidence includes paid ranking links, automated placements and documented exchanges made for ranking purposes. A suspicious-looking page alone does not establish that your site has been penalised.</p>
  <p>For each concern, record what happened, who arranged it, whether payment or reciprocal placement was involved and which URLs are affected. Check the Search Console Manual Actions report. If the only evidence is a vendor's toxicity score, leave the item as unconfirmed rather than converting the score into a removal instruction.</p>

  <h2 id="disavow-process">When is disavowing links appropriate?</h2>
  <p>Google says most sites do not need its disavow tool. Its <a href="https://support.google.com/webmasters/answer/2648487">disavow guidance</a> requires both a substantial artificial or low-quality link problem and a resulting or likely manual action. Attempt removal where appropriate first. A traffic decline or an automated toxicity alert alone does not meet that test.</p>
  <p>If the criteria apply, have an experienced owner review the exact scope and existing file before upload. A replacement file supersedes the previous one; a domain-wide entry affects more than one page. The tool uses supported URL-prefix properties rather than Domain properties. Follow Google's current format and property instructions, retain a versioned copy and document the decision.</p>
  <p>Later ranking changes do not prove the disavow caused them. Record other site changes, demand shifts and search updates when reviewing the outcome.</p>

  <h2 id="link-reclamation">Recover useful links by fixing the destination</h2>
  <p><strong>Illustrative finding:</strong> an industry resource links to your old research URL, which now returns 404, while the same research exists at a new URL. Verify that the new page is the genuine replacement, add a direct redirect and retest the final response. Do not redirect every expired page to the homepage.</p>
  <p>If a publisher removed a reference, check whether your content is still useful before requesting an update. An unlinked mention may merit a polite suggestion, but the publisher controls its editorial decision. Record the source, proposed destination and reader benefit rather than promising a recovery rate.</p>

  <h2 id="building-better-links">Turn the audit into an evidence-based action list</h2>
  <p>Prioritise broken destinations you control, documented policy issues and resources that deserve maintenance. Original research, working tools and clearly sourced explanations can give other publishers a reason to reference your site; they do not guarantee links, rankings or AI citations.</p>
  <p>Use the <a href="/resources/agency-audit-toolkit/">blank findings register</a> to record evidence and owners. The <a href="/competitor-website-audit/">competitor audit workflow</a> can help identify relevant resource formats, while the <a href="/website-audit-services/report-example/">completed report example</a> shows how to connect a finding to a fix and a retest.</p>

</ContentLayout>
